            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "namespaces are related but I think that they're nearly orthogonal. My understanding of namespaces is that it's about enabling multiple plugins to map the same key and having behavior besides \"last map applied wins\" or \"error because you didn't say you were remapping\". Prefix groups is about giving a name to an intermediate node in the nominal state machine (I don't think neovim actually models it this way, but conceptually) that decides what to do with some user input.\n\ne.g. suppose I have three bindings to `<leader>ax`, `<leader>ay`, and `<leader>az`. When I press `<leader>`, a plugin like which-key or mini.clue has to show something for what happens when I press `a`. The group or clue gives that intermediate state you're in after `<leader>a` a name which can be shown in that hint instead of just saying that there's 3 bindings.\n\nI think that the overlap between the two feature happens when you have conflicts between prefixes. If I install two plugins with default bindings and both put them behind `<leader>a`, the namespace to pull commands from could be selected at that point rather than when I finish. And without doing that any hints will be confusing, especially if the design you sketched where Nvim opens up a menu of namespaces after you finish typing the command is selected.",
